# Concept: the command log, undo and restore
#
# Every accepted write is recorded. One op call is one command group: one
# transaction, one actor, one correlation id.
#   - A group holds one command per row touched, with before and after images.
#   - Groups form a hash chain per tenant (hash = SHA-256(prev || body)).
#   - Nothing is ever edited. Undo appends the inverse as NEW history.
#
# `transfer` below writes two rows, so its group has two commands. Reverting
# the group undoes both together.
#
#   ordo revert examples/concepts/06-undo-and-history.ordo \
#       --space spc_acme --group <correlation id> --as ops:ada --dry-run
#
# `ordo restore --record` is a different act: it records that the whole database
# was restored from a backup, so the platform serves the tenant again.
#
# Read next: docs/howto-audit.md, docs/howto-operating.md "Backup and Restore".

domain bank @ 1.0.0

entity accounts {
  holder:  text
  balance: money = 0
  check balance >= 0
}

entity transfers {
  fromAccount -> accounts
  toAccount   -> accounts
  amount:     money
  note:       text = ""
}

# One atomic group: a ledger row and two balance changes. All three land
# or none does, and one revert takes all three back.
op transfer($from: id, $to: id, $amount: money) =
  set transfers { fromAccount: $from, toAccount: $to, amount: $amount }
  set accounts(id == $from) { balance: balance - $amount }
  set accounts(id == $to) { balance: balance + $amount }

op ledger unbounded =
  transfers[amount, note, fromAccount.holder, toAccount.holder]
  sort -id
